virglrenderer: Venus submits only primaries the guest ended, whose secondaries are what they executed

What: a command buffer gets a serial, one more at each begin, reset and pool reset (also
of a pool's freed secondaries a primary still holds), whatever the driver's call returns;
an ended flag, set when the guest ends it (whatever the driver returns) and cleared by a
begin, reset, pool reset or free; and the driver's vkEndCommandBuffer result.
vkCmdExecuteCommands keeps the serial of each secondary in the primary's held entry
(virgl-venus-secondary-holds.patch). vkQueueSubmit and vkQueueSubmit2 refuse, before any
driver call, a command buffer that is not a primary the guest ended, and a primary whose
secondary was begun, reset or had its pool reset since the primary executed it; the
primary's vkEndCommandBuffer refuses the latter too. A command buffer whose driver end
failed goes on as before on MoltenVK (one line per app); on other drivers its submit, or
the vkCmdExecuteCommands of such a secondary, is refused. A refusal ends the app's
context. Vulkan calls all of the refused cases invalid, so a legal app never meets them
(a driver that cannot record a command is the driver's limit, not the app's error).
Why: both drivers read a secondary's commands after vkCmdExecuteCommands. MoltenVK encodes
the secondary's current content when it encodes the primary (at vkQueueSubmit, at the
primary's vkEndCommandBuffer with MVK_CONFIG_PREFILL_METAL_COMMAND_BUFFERS);
KosmicKrisp replays it at a reusable primary's 2nd and later submit (kk_queue.c,
vk_command_buffer.c). vkr checked the Metal passes a secondary opens and the pass it
continues at the execute (virgl-venus-encoder-cap.patch, virgl-venus-command-state.patch),
so a secondary recorded again with more passes got past the cap below which Metal keeps
QEMU alive. Nothing checked what was submitted: MoltenVK refuses a secondary and a 2nd
submit of a one-time buffer itself (MVKCommandBuffer::canExecute), KosmicKrisp relies on
the app. Both drivers read secondaries inside vkQueueSubmit on the calling thread (MoltenVK's
MVK_CONFIG_SYNCHRONOUS_QUEUE_SUBMITS is 1, KosmicKrisp submits in Mesa's immediate mode),
which the holds rely on (#676).
"Ended" is the guest's call because the guest never sees the driver's end result: Mesa's
Venus driver sends the end without a reply and returns VK_SUCCESS (vn_command_buffer.c).
The first version (#706, taken out of 3.2.23 by #712) used the driver's result and refused
legal submits on MoltenVK: its vkEndCommandBuffer returns VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT when
it could not record one command, e.g. vkCmdResolveImage of an integer format (Metal
resolves none; dEQP-VK renderpasses.*.self_dep_*_ms), and the app hung. MoltenVK skips a
primary whose end failed at the submit (canExecute: nothing of it is encoded, its fence
still signals) and encodes a secondary without the command it dropped (vkr weighed them
all), so both go on there. Mesa (KosmicKrisp) marks such a buffer invalid but checks that
only in debug builds (vk_queue.c), so it would run what it recorded up to the failure.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (VUID-vkQueueSubmit-pCommandBuffers-00070, -00071, -00072,
VUID-vkEndCommandBuffer-commandBuffer-00059).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (c-state-secondary-held-resubmit-ok, -rerecord-ok,
-rerecord-submit, -reset-submit, -pool-reset-submit, -free-pool-reset-submit,
-rerecord-end, s-submit-secondary, -recording, -initial, h-release-secondary-ok,
c-state-end-failed-submit-ok, -resubmit-ok, -secondary-ok, -reset-submit, -rebegin-submit,
c-state-end-hook-submit, -secondary, -reset-ok; also under libgmalloc). The -hook- cases
make an end fail with vkr_test_end_result_hook (NULL in QEMU).

